ercent of cookies that contain fewer than $12$ grams of sugar, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution, *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.900\\,$ | $\\,0.950\\,$ | $\\,0.975\\,$ | $\\,0.990\\,$ | $\\,0.995\\,$ | $\\,0.999\\,$ | $\\,0.9995\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.9600\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ | $\\,2.5758\\,$ | $\\,3.0902\\,$ | $\\,3.2905\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0287\\,$ | $\\,0.0281\\,$ | $\\,0.0274\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083974 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9debd84a-b5d9-54c6-84fa-986684bdfc9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083974 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083974 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the number of grams of sugar in a cookie. Then we have $X∼N(15,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<12).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>17)=0.1.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 17) & =1-P(X>17) \\\\  & =1-0.1 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 17) & =0.9 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{17-15}{\\sigma }) & =0.9 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.9 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.9)≈1.2816.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.6408 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.6408} \\\\  & ≈1.5605.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<12)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<12) & =P(X\\le 12) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{12-15}{1.5605}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.92) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.92) \\\\  & =0.0274 \\\\  & ≈2.7%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $2.7%$ of cookies contain less than $12$ grams of sugar." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083975 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083975 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083975 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083975 #edb/ref 1071 "The test scores of a group of students are normally distributed with a mean of $73$ and a standard deviation of $5.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected student got a score of $80$ or more? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9332\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083975 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e774eae-3ccc-5f1e-a35b-4ce8e1ddec45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083975 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083975 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the score of a randomly selected student. Then we have $X∼N(73,{5}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X\\ge 80).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 80) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{80-73}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 1.4) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.4) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1.40)=1-0.9192≈0.08.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student got $80$ or more is $0.08.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084269 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084269 #edb/ref 1061 30927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084269 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "932c4862-d05e-5b90-85c8-d54c3a5130a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084269 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084270 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084270 #edb/ref 1061 30928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084270 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37a09439-27ba-50b5-9e58-473fafe4e323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084270 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084271 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084271 #edb/ref 1061 30929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084271 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd4c06eb-e846-5f38-b100-a7f13c969c43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084271 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084272 #edb/ref 1061 30930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084272 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "828a2000-0cb2-577a-9a4c-50d319487a43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084272 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084297 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084297 #edb/ref 1061 37200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084297 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "98535c60-8311-53a8-9f73-e175c3ec6497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084297 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084482 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $X\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ is normally distributed. Then the probability density function $f(x)$ of $X$ is a unimodal, bell-shaped curve. Moreover, it is symmetrical about the mean, median, and mode, which all equal $\\mu$.  \n\n![](images/0c/0c8951446f52cea9db6c0a2b600fc53cb564c3d64ff0afab15c9756dc0c0f29b.png)\n\nWe can use the normal distribution to model a wide range of real-life situations because many real-world distributions are roughly bell-shaped and symmetrical around a mean value. \n\nPatients' blood pressures, people's weights, measurement errors, and IQ scores are just some of the real-world examples that can be modeled as a normal distribution. \n\nTo determine whether a given data is normal, the first thing to do is to check the shape of the distribution. For example, if a data set is roughly bell-shaped, then it *might* be normally distributed. \n\nIt should be noted that, in practice, statisticians will often need to perform several tests on the data to check for normality (these are called **normality tests**). We cannot rely only on a distribution's shape since other distributions are also bell-shaped. However, checking the shape is often a good starting point." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084482 #edb/ref 1065 4966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084482 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9f150bce-c1b4-502c-bcb1-da067550b958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084482 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266433 #edb/ref 1080 "8.1%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266433 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266433 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "209a5dc2-51b8-5cf8-a3fd-432567049203" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266502 #edb/ref 1080 "7.9%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266502 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266502 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "285a1d46-eca2-5392-87b2-9ae6a067d406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266633 #edb/ref 1080 "11.5%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266633 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266633 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "383eb090-4034-58c4-90ea-549e44d474a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267243 #edb/ref 1080 "9.6%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267243 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267243 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7be87196-7809-5fb8-a83b-62481b462d15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267828 #edb/ref 1080 "8.9%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267828 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186267828 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc16961d-f939-5b46-a020-4871a6a9d710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186266633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186267828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186266502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186268415 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0642bb25-91a1-5589-b640-3a5b72872474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300674 #edb/ref 1080 "0.51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300674 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300674 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8ce3962b-9372-53e8-94dd-19aae1e9b687" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300689 #edb/ref 1080 "0.10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300689 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186300689 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8fb6f7f4-57a5-52f7-8e64-395b91ab0e51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301589 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301589 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301589 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301589 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186300674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186301589 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "752c40b7-22f4-5848-b34a-2e00b4e7dbd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302031 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186300689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302031 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302031 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302031 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186300689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302031 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fc161ec3-c1d9-5eb1-baaa-2ab67d18a5ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400674 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400674 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400674 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400674 #edb/ref 1071 "The lifetime of a lithium-ion battery produced by a particular company is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ years and a standard deviation of $0.5$ years.\nAdditionally, $3%$ of batteries last less than $1.75$ years. Find the mean $\\mu $, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400674 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42e998b4-da64-533d-80e4-6303495d71cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400674 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the lifetime of a randomly selected battery. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.5}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<1.75)=0.03.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.75) & =0.03 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5}) & =0.03 \\\\ \\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.03).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.03)=-1.8808.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1.75-\\mu }{0.5} & =-1.8808 \\\\ 1.75-\\mu  & =-0.9404 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈2.69.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400675 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400675 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400675 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400675 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400675 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400675 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400675 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400675 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186400676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400675 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eb566678-fdf6-552d-aad6-4bf9b9328ba8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400676 #edb/ref 1080 "$2.69$ years" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400676 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400676 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6938ada6-d242-5a29-b3cc-1005ff1beb10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400677 #edb/ref 1080 "$2.74$ years" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400677 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400677 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f4c15084-f362-505f-8950-6df3b2548ace" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400678 #edb/ref 1080 "$2.65$ years" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400678 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400678 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e01f491c-202f-5132-a3f5-3f0f2930aa15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400679 #edb/ref 1080 "$2.57$ years" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400679 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400679 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "79ea6bf2-0abb-5dd3-8132-52b2488943a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400680 #edb/ref 1080 "$2.79$ years" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400680 #edb/ref 1082 #ed