075 #edb/ref 17592186507145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507383 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507383 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507383 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507383 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eb3c213e-a427-5f30-8b6a-3f5985900ded" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507444 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507444 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507444 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507444 #edb/ref 1071 "The price of a deli sandwich is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$1.10.$ In addition, in $90%$ of cases the price is between $$0.80\\mu $ and $$1.20\\mu .$ Find the average price, **rounding your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$dollars$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507444 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62a29958-3d43-5575-a73a-6517a1734f8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507444 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent a sandwich price. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{1.10}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu )=0.90.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.45 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.5+0.45 \\\\ P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)=1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & =1.6449 \\\\ 0.20\\mu  & =1.809\\,39 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.809\\,39}{0.20} \\\\  & ≈9.05\\,\\text{dollars}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-325117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507489 #edb/ref 1071 "The heights of oak trees in a park are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ feet and a standard deviation of $8$ feet. Additionally, $25%$ of the trees are shorter than $45$ feet. Find the percentage of trees taller than $50$ feet. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ | $\\,0.4443\\,$ | $\\,0.4404\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.4880\\,$ | $\\,0.4840\\,$ | $\\,0.4801\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd63f548-8bbe-5de7-9356-fc14587f7fab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507489 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected tree. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>50).$  \n\nWe know that $P(X<45)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<45) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 45-\\mu  & =-5.396 \\\\ \\mu  & =50.396.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(50<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>50) & =P(Z>\\frac{50-50.396}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.05) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.05) \\\\  & =1-0.4801 \\\\  & ≈51.99%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $51.99%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507553 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507553 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5bce04ca-70c6-54fe-8fc6-e22eeb0734f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507553 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507572 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507572 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7e8932f-d2fc-59f3-abbd-cd780ac112de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507572 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507574 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507574 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c58cd6d2-7a47-5d68-ba83-ba5b2c86397f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507574 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186400674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507584 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507584 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de38d27b-caa8-5826-b9f1-c99bfdb